{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the MLP with a Transformer encoder, add gradual unfreezing, and apply mixup augmentation. Historical experiments show that Transformer architectures with careful regularization can improve generalization on small text datasets. The current code executes the MLP baseline but suffers from overfitting (train acc 1.0, val log loss ~0.44). The three modules selected are where all modifications must be applied: model_design for the Transformer architecture, training_evaluation for gradual unfreezing and mixup, and data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not changed but kept for consistency with the plan.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current FeatureClassifier (MLP) with a Transformer encoder operating on dense feature embeddings. Specifically, after feature selection, project the sparse feature vector (13035 dims) through a learnable linear embedding (256 dims), add positional encoding (same dimension), and pass through 2 Transformer encoder layers (4 heads, hidden dim 512, dropout 0.1). Use a mean pooling layer over the sequence of hidden states, followed by a linear classification head to 3 outputs. Keep the input dimension variable as before. This change is based on the initial plan's rationale and successful use of Transformer for feature interaction in similar historical experiments.", "training_evaluation": "Implement gradual unfreezing of the Transformer layers: initially freeze the embedding layer and first Transformer layer, train only the final classification head and last Transformer layer for 5 epochs, then progressively unfreeze one additional layer every 3 epochs with a cosine annealing restart scheduler resetting upon each unfreeze. Additionally, apply mixup augmentation on the feature space during training: for each batch, sample λ ~ Beta(0.4,0.4), create mixed feature vectors and linear interpolated labels (one-hot), and use standard cross-entropy loss on soft labels. Use OneCycleLR scheduler with peak lr=5e-4 and warmup ratio=0.1. Keep early stopping patience=10, 50 total epochs, batch size 128, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, label smoothing 0.1, AdamW optimizer with weight_decay=1e-4. This integrates the initial plan's gradual unfreezing and mixup, while being informed by the overfitting patterns observed in the current run."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the MLP with a Transformer encoder, add gradual unfreezing, and apply mixup augmentation. Historical experiments show that Transformer architectures with careful regularization can improve generalization on small text datasets. The current code executes the MLP baseline but suffers from overfitting (train acc 1.0, val log loss ~0.44). The three modules selected are where all modifications must be applied: model_design for the Transformer architecture, training_evaluation for gradual unfreezing and mixup, and data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not changed but kept for consistency with the plan.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current FeatureClassifier (MLP) with a Transformer encoder operating on dense feature embeddings. Specifically, after feature selection, project the sparse feature vector (13035 dims) through a learnable linear embedding (256 dims), add positional encoding (same dimension), and pass through 2 Transformer encoder layers (4 heads, hidden dim 512, dropout 0.1). Use a mean pooling layer over the sequence of hidden states, followed by a linear classification head to 3 outputs. Keep the input dimension variable as before. This change is based on the initial plan's rationale and successful use of Transformer for feature interaction in similar historical experiments.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement gradual unfreezing of the Transformer layers: initially freeze the embedding layer and first Transformer layer, train only the final classification head and last Transformer layer for 5 epochs, then progressively unfreeze one additional layer every 3 epochs with a cosine annealing restart scheduler resetting upon each unfreeze. Additionally, apply mixup augmentation on the feature space during training: for each batch, sample λ ~ Beta(0.4,0.4), create mixed feature vectors and linear interpolated labels (one-hot), and use standard cross-entropy loss on soft labels. Use OneCycleLR scheduler with peak lr=5e-4 and warmup ratio=0.1. Keep early stopping patience=10, 50 total epochs, batch size 128, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, label smoothing 0.1, AdamW optimizer with weight_decay=1e-4. This integrates the initial plan's gradual unfreezing and mixup, while being informed by the overfitting patterns observed in the current run."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan aims to improve Transformer usage by creating feature-group tokens and removing problematic training techniques (mixup, gradual unfreezing). Historical successful experiments used simple MLPs without mixup/unfreezing and achieved stable convergence. Failed experiments with Transformer+gradual unfreezing+mixup showed unstable validation curves similar to current run (epochs 1-5 random, then sudden jump, fluctuating loss). Thus, refining model_design for the feature-group token approach and training_evaluation to remove mixup/unfreezing is most promising. data_processing_and_feature_engineering is kept as-is since feature pipeline is adequate.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify FeatureTransformer to process the input as 3 feature-group tokens instead of 1 token. Segment the 13035-dimensional vector into word n-gram TF-IDF (8000), char n-gram TF-IDF (5000), and stylometric+stats (35). Project each group via separate linear layers (no bias) to d_model=256, producing a 3-token sequence. Add positional encoding and pass through 2 Transformer encoder layers (4 heads, dim_feedforward=512, dropout=0.1, GELU). Use mean pooling over the 3 tokens before classification head. Remove the mixup-related code and ensure compatibility with standard cross-entropy loss.", "training_evaluation": "Remove mixup augmentation and gradual unfreezing. Keep standard CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1. Train all layers from epoch 1 with AdamW optimizer (lr=5e-4, weight_decay=1e-4, betas=(0.9,0.999)). Replace OneCycleLR with ReduceLROnPlateau (mode='min', factor=0.5, patience=3, min_lr=1e-6). Keep batch size 128, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, early stopping patience=10 epochs based on validation log loss, and best model checkpointing. This stabilizes training by avoiding disruptive gradient shifts from unfreezing and unrealistic augmented samples."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan aims to improve Transformer usage by creating feature-group tokens and removing problematic training techniques (mixup, gradual unfreezing). Historical successful experiments used simple MLPs without mixup/unfreezing and achieved stable convergence. Failed experiments with Transformer+gradual unfreezing+mixup showed unstable validation curves similar to current run (epochs 1-5 random, then sudden jump, fluctuating loss). Thus, refining model_design for the feature-group token approach and training_evaluation to remove mixup/unfreezing is most promising. data_processing_and_feature_engineering is kept as-is since feature pipeline is adequate.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify FeatureTransformer to process the input as 3 feature-group tokens instead of 1 token. Segment the 13035-dimensional vector into word n-gram TF-IDF (8000), char n-gram TF-IDF (5000), and stylometric+stats (35). Project each group via separate linear layers (no bias) to d_model=256, producing a 3-token sequence. Add positional encoding and pass through 2 Transformer encoder layers (4 heads, dim_feedforward=512, dropout=0.1, GELU). Use mean pooling over the 3 tokens before classification head. Remove the mixup-related code and ensure compatibility with standard cross-entropy loss.",
    "training_evaluation": "Remove mixup augmentation and gradual unfreezing. Keep standard CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1. Train all layers from epoch 1 with AdamW optimizer (lr=5e-4, weight_decay=1e-4, betas=(0.9,0.999)). Replace OneCycleLR with ReduceLROnPlateau (mode='min', factor=0.5, patience=3, min_lr=1e-6). Keep batch size 128, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, early stopping patience=10 epochs based on validation log loss, and best model checkpointing. This stabilizes training by avoiding disruptive gradient shifts from unfreezing and unrealistic augmented samples."
  }
}"}

